title: Builder
manual: structure/#c2-edit

Modify the structure shown in the view by clicking on it: add or
remove atoms and fragments, change the number of hydrogens, create or
remove bonds, and edit distances, angles, and dihedrals. To use the
builder, choose a tool from the palette; the panel below explains what
a click in the view does and how to stop. Undo in the Edit menu
(by default Control-z) reverts a change.

# Tools
- Add: "Add Atoms" opens the periodic table; the chosen element is
  placed with the selected local geometry (linear, bent, tetrahedral,
  ...), and clicking an atom replaces it, keeping the non-hydrogen
  substituents. "Add Fragments" opens the fragment library; clicking
  an atom bonds the fragment to it (ligands) or replaces the atom, and
  clicking empty space places the fragment there. The "Recent:" allows
  reselecting previously used atoms or fragments.
- Valence: "Change Valence" adds or removes hydrogens on the clicked atom.
- Remove: "Remove Atoms" deletes the clicked atoms and their terminal hydrogens; "Trim
  Branches" also deletes every fragment left disconnected.
- Bonds: "Create Bonds" bonds two atoms clicked one after the other;
  "Create Bonds (-H)" also removes a terminal hydrogen from each;
  "Remove Bonds" removes the clicked bond; "Bond Order" cycles it
  through single, double, triple, aromatic and dashed.
- Edit: select two, three or four atoms in the view, then press the button to change
  their distance, angle or dihedral, choosing which atoms move. "Apply" commits and
  "Revert" restores the starting value. When measuring in the view,
  pressing "E" allows editing a distance, angle, or dihedral.
- Symmetry: moves the atoms onto their symmetry positions exactly; the
  point group or space group is shown next to the button.
- Relax: relaxes the geometry to the nearest minimum with the chosen force field until
  the maximum force drops below "Fmax (eV/Å)". Only the atoms selected in the view move;
  with no selection, all of the atoms move.
- "Rebond" recomputes the connectivity, discarding the bonds created or removed by hand;
  "Restore" reloads the original geometry, discarding every edit.
- The "Atoms", "Cell", "Bonds", "Symmetry" and "Dynamics" buttons open those windows.

# Notes
- An error from any tool appears in red at the bottom of the window.
